    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the total surface area of the pyramid, we need to consider both the base area and the lateral area (the four triangular faces), by summing them up. Initially, the detailed process involves breaking down larger calculations into basic concepts and applying fundamental properties in geometry.\n\n### Step 1: Identifying the Base Area Of The Pyramid\n\nThe base of the pyramid is a square with each side having a length of \\(10 \\,\\text{cm}\\). Thus, the formula for the area of a square is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the square base} = \\text{side}^2\n\\]\n\nPlugging in the values, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the square base} = 10^2 = 100 \\,\\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculating Each Face of the Pyramid\n\nSince the base is a square, there are four identical triangular faces meeting at each corner of the square. To calculate the area of one of these triangular faces, we begin by determining its height, which is the perpendicular distance from that face to the center of the base. \n\nThe height of each triangular face is given as \\(12 \\,\\text{cm}\\). \n\n### Step 3: Calculating The Lateral Face Area\n\nWe use the formula for the area of a triangle, which is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of a triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\n\\]\n\nHere, the base of each triangle is the same as the side of the square base, i.e., \\(10 \\,\\text{cm}\\), and the height is \\(12 \\,\\text{cm}\\). Plugging these values into the formula gives:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\,\\text{cm} \\times 12 \\,\\text{cm} = 60 \\,\\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\nSince there are four such triangular faces, the total lateral area is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total lateral area} = 4 \\times 60 \\,\\text{cm}^2 = 240 \\,\\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Finding The Total Surface Area\n\nThe total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the square base and the lateral area calculated above:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total surface area} = \\text{Area of the square base} + \\text{Total lateral area} = 100 \\,\\text{cm}^2 + 240 \\,\\text{cm}^2 = 340 \\,\\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the right pyramid is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{340 \\,\\text{cm}^2}\n\\]",
